{"name":"Castle Wall","hex":"#c2bba2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"castle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large residential building or compound that is fortified and contains many defences; in previous ages often inhabited by a nobleman or king. Also, a house or mansion with some of the architectural features of medieval castles.","An instance of castling."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To house or keep in a castle.","To protect or separate in a similar way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/castle"},{"term":"wall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rampart of earth, stones etc. built up for defensive purposes.","A structure built for defense surrounding a city, castle etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose with, or as if with, a wall or walls.","To use a wallhack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Curtain wall (fortification)","blocks":[{"text":"A curtain wall is a defensive wall between fortified towers or bastions of a castle, fortress, or town."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Beaumaris_aerial.jpg/330px-Beaumaris_aerial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curtain_wall_(fortification)"},{"title":"Castle","blocks":[{"text":"A castle is a type of fortified structure built during the Middle Ages predominantly by the nobility or royalty and by military orders. Scholars usually consider a castle to be the private fortified residence of a lord or noble. This is distinct from a mansion, palace, and villa, whose main purpose was exclusively for pleasance and are not primarily fortresses but may be fortified. Use of the term has varied over time and, sometimes, has also been applied to structures such as hill forts and 19th- and 20th-century homes built to resemble castles. Over the Middle Ages, when genuine castles were built, they took on a great many forms with many different features, although some, such as curtain walls, arrowslits, and portcullises, were commonplace."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Panor%C3%A1mica_Oto%C3%B1o_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Segovia.jpg/330px-Panor%C3%A1mica_Oto%C3%B1o_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Segovia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle"},{"title":"Wall","blocks":[{"text":"A wall is a structure and a surface that defines an area; carries a load; provides security, shelter, or soundproofing; or serves a decorative purpose. There are various types of walls, including border barriers between countries, brick walls, defensive walls in fortifications, and retaining walls that hold back dirt, stone, water, or noise. Walls can also be found in buildings, where they support roofs, floors, and ceilings, enclose spaces, and provide shelter and security."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg/330px-Noel_2005_P%C3%A9kin_031_muraille_de_chine_Mutianyu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wall"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/General_view_of_Lachish%2CTell_ed_Duweir%2C_looking_east_Wellcome_L0005979.jpg/500px-General_view_of_Lachish%2CTell_ed_Duweir%2C_looking_east_Wellcome_L0005979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/General_view_of_Lachish%2CTell_ed_Duweir%2C_looking_east_Wellcome_L0005979.jpg/1280px-General_view_of_Lachish%2CTell_ed_Duweir%2C_looking_east_Wellcome_L0005979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/General_view_of_Lachish%2CTell_ed_Duweir%2C_looking_east_Wellc